  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "Can a line through the origin be written in intercept form \\(x/a+y/b=1\\)?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "Yes, as long as its slope is 1",
    "B": "No, at least one of the two intercepts is 0",
    "C": "Yes, by setting both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) to 0",
    "D": "No, because the origin has no coordinates"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Intercept form requires both intercepts to be nonzero. For a line through the origin, at least one intercept is 0.\n\nAlternative method 1: Substitute the origin\n\nFor \\(x/a+y/b=1\\) to be defined, \\(a\\ne0\\) and \\(b\\ne0\\). A line through the origin must contain \\((0,0)\\), but substituting these coordinates gives \\(0/a+0/b=0\\), whereas the equation requires the left side to equal \\(1\\). Thus no line through the origin has this intercept form. The answer is B.",
